{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RX4fQYSt7i1yxBRz7qdSom","type":"tool_result","content":"**Exploren prices on this page**\n\nThe page doesn't split prices into DC fast and AC charging. Only one site is clearly labelled as DC:\n\n- **UTAS Shed Building - Public DC (Launceston, TAS):** \"70c/kWh base\". The idle fee is 100c/min after a 10-minute grace period, and the connection fee is $0.00.\n\nThe other three named sites aren't identified by charger type:\n\n- Toronto Commercial Centre Carpark, NSW: 35c/kWh, plus a $0.30 connection fee\n- Kingston Village Square, VIC: 55c/kWh\n- Cairns Night Markets, QLD: 60c/kWh\n\nAcross all four sites, the page says energy costs \"$0.35 to $0.70 per kWh\". It gives **no typical or median figure**, only this range. For a 40 kWh stop, that works out to $14.00–$28.00 before fees.\n\n**Dates**\n\nEvery site row was checked on 24 October 2026. One vehicle capacity source oddly shows a check date of 2026-11-13, which is later than the page's stated check date.\n\n**How the data was gathered**\n\nThe prices come from Exploren's public charger-finder listings, with each row linked to its source. The page says it captures \"published source information rather than an account-specific app quote or a visit to the charger.\" None of the sources stated time bands or member pricing, and parking charges weren't verified."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b400f4c1-5085-47c3-af7a-8e6a57ec0ba2","uuid":"05654bda-e035-4292-944c-d49c843ef6e8","timestamp":"2026-11-19T11:45:51.217Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":21692,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Exploren prices on this page**\n\nThe page doesn't split prices into DC fast and AC charging. Only one site is clearly labelled as DC:\n\n- **UTAS Shed Building - Public DC (Launceston, TAS):** \"70c/kWh base\". The idle fee is 100c/min after a 10-minute grace period, and the connection fee is $0.00.\n\nThe other three named sites aren't identified by charger type:\n\n- Toronto Commercial Centre Carpark, NSW: 35c/kWh, plus a $0.30 connection fee\n- Kingston Village Square, VIC: 55c/kWh\n- Cairns Night Markets, QLD: 60c/kWh\n\nAcross all four sites, the page says energy costs \"$0.35 to $0.70 per kWh\". It gives **no typical or median figure**, only this range. For a 40 kWh stop, that works out to $14.00–$28.00 before fees.\n\n**Dates**\n\nEvery site row was checked on 24 October 2026. One vehicle capacity source oddly shows a check date of 2026-11-13, which is later than the page's stated check date.\n\n**How the data was gathered**\n\nThe prices come from Exploren's public charger-finder listings, with each row linked to its source. The page says it captures \"published source information rather than an account-specific app quote or a visit to the charger.\" None of the sources stated time bands or member pricing, and parking charges weren't verified.","durationMs":7282,"url":"https://costtocharge.com.au/public-charging-prices/exploren/"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RX4fQYSt7i1yxBRz7qdSom","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AFX9JwEaotDvMnwiXE457g","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"EVSE Exploren acquires ENGIE fast charging network Australia sites Exploren app\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"EVSE Acquires ENGIE's Fast Charging Network, Boosts Exploren Footprint\",\"url\":\"https://zecar.com/reviews/evse-acquires-engie-s-fast-charging-network-boosts-exploren-footprint\"},{\"title\":\"EVSE acquires Engie’s Australian and New Zealand EV charging network\",\"url\":\"https://thedriven.io/2025/04/08/evse-acquires-engies-australian-and-new-zealand-ev-charging-network/\"},{\"title\":\"EVSE adds 200+ charge points with acquisition of ENGIE charging network\",\"url\":\"https://electricdrives.tv/evse-adds-200-charge-points-with-acquisition-of-engie-charging-network/\"},{\"title\":\"Intellihub's EVSE acquires ENGIE ANZ charging network\",\"url\":\"https://esdnews.com.au/intellihubs-evse-acquires-engie-anz-charging-network/\"},{\"title\":\"EV charging company to invest $30 million in expanding its Australian network, including 600 new fast chargers - The Driven IO\",\"url\":\"https://thedriven.io/2026/09/26/ev-charging-company-to-invest-30-million-in-expanding-its-australian-network-including-600-new-fast-chargers/#038;utm_medium=rss&\"},{\"title\":\"EVSE Expands Australia-NZ Charging Network with ENGIE Acquisition - Kurrant\",\"url\":\"https://kurrant.com/kurrantly-news/evse-expands-australia-nz-charging-network-with-engie-acquisition/\"},{\"title\":\"Segment Y Automotive Intelligence: EVSE expands charging network by 230 locations with ENGIE acquisition\",\"url\":\"https://segmenty.com/GenerateNewsStaticJsps/EVSE_expands_charging_network_by_230_locations_with_ENGIE_acquisition.html\"},{\"title\":\"Exploren Becomes ACT’s Largest EV Charging Network - Fleet EV News\",\"url\":\"https://fleetevnews.com.au/exploren-becomes-acts-largest-ev-charging-network/\"},{\"title\":\"Exploren: Charging Network Guide for EV Drivers\",\"url\":\"https://evroadside.com.au/networks/exploren/\"},{\"title\":\"EV Charging Networks in Australia\",\"url\":\"https://evrunningcost.com.au/tools/ev-charging-networks\"}]\n\nEVSE, part of the Intellihub Group, bought ENGIE's Australian and New Zealand EV charging network in April 2025 and is moving it onto its Exploren network and app.\n\n**The deal**\n- EVSE acquired the Australian and New Zealand EV charging network of global energy and services group ENGIE for an undisclosed sum. According to the AFR the amount was less than $50 million.\n- The acquisition was reportedly supported by Australia's oldest private equity firm Pacific Equity Partners and American investing giant Brookfield.\n- Intellihub also entered into a landmark long term commercial partnership with ENGIE to roll out home smart energy devices.\n\n**How big it is (sources disagree)**\n- A statement quoted by Zecar described the network as \"more than 230 DC charge ports at over 80 sites, with a lot more being built at multiple locations\".\n- The press coverage instead says the deal adds more than 200 new locations, and Kurrant says over 230 high-traffic locations.\n- The EV Roadside guide sides with the \"230+ DC ports across 80+ sites\" version. The \"200+ locations\" claim may count ports as sites.\n\n**Sites**\n- They include Chadstone, Australia's largest shopping centre, Chatswood Chase and Homebush DFO in Sydney.\n- The DC chargers can top up a battery in as little as 30 minutes.\n\n**Rebrand and the Exploren app**\n- In the statement, EVSE said it would spend the following months rebranding the network to Exploren, and all of the chargers will begin to appear in the Exploren app. The entire Engie Infrastructure Team was joining as well.\n- The deal shrinks the size of the Chargefox network. Until now, Exploren was mainly a network for AC chargers owned by shopping malls, fleets, and holiday accommodation providers.\n- CEO Brendan Wheeler said Exploren gives drivers a singular payment interface for sites across the country.\n\n**What's happened since**\n- In late 2025, Exploren became the ACT's largest network, with 93 charging ports now operating across 27 locations.\n- It now claims more than 5,000 charge points across Australia and New Zealand, used by around 90,000 drivers a month. Chargers can be started by app or RFID.\n- In September 2026, Exploren committed $30 million over the next two years to expand its charging network and add more than 600 new DC fast chargers. It is also adding contactless payment so drivers don't need multiple app downloads before charging.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b400f4c1-5085-47c3-af7a-8e6a57ec0ba2","uuid":"d9d24f9b-15b6-47ce-996c-c78bf83d7513","timestamp":"2026-11-19T11:45:54.788Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"EVSE Exploren acquires ENGIE fast charging network Australia sites Exploren app","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_012pDWZK6KAGwCRKPbA8vvNr","content":[{"title":"EVSE Acquires ENGIE's Fast Charging Network, Boosts Exploren Footprint","url":"https://zecar.com/reviews/evse-acquires-engie-s-fast-charging-network-boosts-exploren-footprint"},{"title":"EVSE acquires Engie’s Australian and New Zealand EV charging network","url":"https://thedriven.io/2025/04/08/evse-acquires-engies-australian-and-new-zealand-ev-charging-network/"},{"title":"EVSE adds 200+ charge points with acquisition of ENGIE charging network","url":"https://electricdrives.tv/evse-adds-200-charge-points-with-acquisition-of-engie-charging-network/"},{"title":"Intellihub's EVSE acquires ENGIE ANZ charging network","url":"https://esdnews.com.au/intellihubs-evse-acquires-engie-anz-charging-network/"},{"title":"EV charging company to invest $30 million in expanding its Australian network, including 600 new fast chargers - The Driven IO","url":"https://thedriven.io/2026/09/26/ev-charging-company-to-invest-30-million-in-expanding-its-australian-network-including-600-new-fast-chargers/#038;utm_medium=rss&"},{"title":"EVSE Expands Australia-NZ Charging Network with ENGIE Acquisition - Kurrant","url":"https://kurrant.com/kurrantly-news/evse-expands-australia-nz-charging-network-with-engie-acquisition/"},{"title":"Segment Y Automotive Intelligence: EVSE expands charging network by 230 locations with ENGIE acquisition","url":"https://segmenty.com/GenerateNewsStaticJsps/EVSE_expands_charging_network_by_230_locations_with_ENGIE_acquisition.html"},{"title":"Exploren Becomes ACT’s Largest EV Charging Network - Fleet EV News","url":"https://fleetevnews.com.au/exploren-becomes-acts-largest-ev-charging-network/"},{"title":"Exploren: Charging Network Guide for EV Drivers","url":"https://evroadside.com.au/networks/exploren/"},{"title":"EV Charging Networks in Australia","url":"https://evrunningcost.com.au/tools/ev-charging-networks"}]},"EVSE, part of the Intellihub Group, bought ENGIE's Australian and New Zealand EV charging network in April 2025 and is moving it onto its Exploren network and app.\n\n**The deal**\n- EVSE acquired the Australian and New Zealand EV charging network of global energy and services group ENGIE for an undisclosed sum. According to the AFR the amount was less than $50 million.\n- The acquisition was reportedly supported by Australia's oldest private equity firm Pacific Equity Partners and American investing giant Brookfield.\n- Intellihub also entered into a landmark long term commercial partnership with ENGIE to roll out home smart energy devices.\n\n**How big it is (sources disagree)**\n- A statement quoted by Zecar described the network as \"more than 230 DC charge ports at over 80 sites, with a lot more being built at multiple locations\".\n- The press coverage instead says the deal adds more than 200 new locations, and Kurrant says over 230 high-traffic locations.\n- The EV Roadside guide sides with the \"230+ DC ports across 80+ sites\" version. The \"200+ locations\" claim may count ports as sites.\n\n**Sites**\n- They include Chadstone, Australia's largest shopping centre, Chatswood Chase and Homebush DFO in Sydney.\n- The DC chargers can top up a battery in as little as 30 minutes.\n\n**Rebrand and the Exploren app**\n- In the statement, EVSE said it would spend the following months rebranding the network to Exploren, and all of the chargers will begin to appear in the Exploren app. The entire Engie Infrastructure Team was joining as well.\n- The deal shrinks the size of the Chargefox network. Until now, Exploren was mainly a network for AC chargers owned by shopping malls, fleets, and holiday accommodation providers.\n- CEO Brendan Wheeler said Exploren gives drivers a singular payment interface for sites across the country.\n\n**What's happened since**\n- In late 2025, Exploren became the ACT's largest network, with 93 charging ports now operating across 27 locations.\n- It now claims more than 5,000 charge points across Australia and New Zealand, used by around 90,000 drivers a month. Chargers can be started by app or RFID.\n- In September 2026, Exploren committed $30 million over the next two years to expand its charging network and add more than 600 new DC fast chargers. It is also adding contactless payment so drivers don't need multiple app downloads before charging."],"durationSeconds":11.960395536999997,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AFX9JwEaotDvMnwiXE457g","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
